# Broccoli Support Knowledge Base

- Search `tickets/` first (verified), then `knowledge-base/`, then `training/`. Each ticket is a JSON
  record; the bs plugin's `kb.py` searches them (`find <words>`, `plan --symptom <tag>`, `tags`).
- If nothing in the KB covers the question, say the answer is unverified. Don't guess.
- Never commit end customers' names, phone numbers, addresses, emails or user IDs. Company names are fine.
- Never send anything to a customer or post to Pylon or Slack.
- After writing a ticket record any way other than `/bs:save-ticket-to-kb`, run the bs plugin's
  `shared/scripts/save_kb_record.sh tickets/<number>.json`. It formats, checks and leak-scans the
  record. The pre-commit hook refuses a record that fails the check.
- Privacy checks cover only this repo and its git history, not Slack or Pylon.

## Always back up teammates

Never blame a coworker in replies or notes. If a coworker gave a client wrong information or a fix that did not work, correct the
information or the fix gracefully without blaming the coworker.

Examples:

- "It looks like `<coworker_name>` made the change, but it's tough to catch every edge case with LLMs."
- "`<coworker_name>` did a great job on this, but LLMs aren't 100% predictable, so here's how we can fine tune the agent: ..."

## ServiceTitan Research

Use this when a question is about where something lives in ServiceTitan, how a setting works, or why data did not reach Broccoli from it. Answer from ServiceTitan's own help center, never from a web search.

1. Fetch the index at https://help.servicetitan.com/llms.txt and search its titles for the topic. (The index is large; grep it rather than read it.)
2. Article Lookups:
  - Articles appear once per edition.
  - Prefer the link under `/docs/`.
  - Only use the edition relevant to the customer's ticket: skip Residential, Commercial, Construction, and Mobile copies.
3. Fetch the article as Markdown by adding `.md` to its URL. Read the `updated` date in its front matter and say it if the article is older than a year.
4. Answer with the exact click path as the article gives it, menu names in bold, one step per line, and name the role the article says the step needs.
5. Give the article's URL at the end so the owner can open it.
6. If the help center does not cover the question, so say. Do NOT fill the gap from memory.

Call journeys, ring targets and who hung up are in Dialpad's Call History, not in ServiceTitan's own call log. Integration settings live under Settings, Integrations, API Application Access, and there is no API for them.

The developer portal at developer.servicetitan.io only renders in a browser. For API questions, download the OpenAPI file from the API's page rather than reading the portal.

## Rules

- Delete with `trash`, never `rm`.
- To read a web page, run `scrapepage <url>`. It reads the tab the owner has open in Dia, or opens the page in Dia and closes it afterwards. chrome-devtools-axi drives that same Dia for anything beyond reading.

## Code

- Code is self-documenting. A comment is the last resort.
- One feature per component. Components talk only through their boundaries. Follow SOLID.
- Keep files small. Split a file before it holds a second feature.
- Code is test-driven: write the failing test first, against the public interface at the feature boundary. Shell scripts and config files are exempt.
- Tests live in their own files, never beside production code.

## Comments

- Comment only what a developer cannot read from the code or setting name. Delete a comment that restates it.
- Say the effect, then any condition it needs, in full sentences. Use a second line only to keep a fact.
- No labels, banners, links, parentheticals, mechanism, or values that drift such as versions and dates. Explain a workaround in words.

Agents use plain git here.

Open, merge or auto-merge a PR only when asked by name.
